      "math_academy_id": "e-773",
      "problem": "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\dfrac 1 4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$",
